+
+# JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) resolvePointer
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document. Implement JSON Pointer as
+specified in RFC 6901.
+
+Export a function `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. Given a JSON document and a
+JSON Pointer string, it returns the value the pointer refers to. It throws an error when the pointer is
+not a valid JSON Pointer, or when it does not resolve to a value of the document.
+
+This is the first of several features that build on each other; later ones will use it.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- The empty pointer `""` returns the whole document.
+- `/a/b` walks object members and array elements; array indices are non-negative integers without
+  leading zeros (except `0` itself); `-` is not a valid index for reading and does not resolve.
+- `~1` decodes to `/` and `~0` decodes to `~`; decoding is applied in that order per RFC 6901 section 4.
+- A pointer that is non-empty and does not start with `/` throws.
+- Invalid escapes (a `~` not followed by `0` or `1`) throw.
+- A reference that does not resolve (missing member, index out of range, traversal into a primitive)
+  throws.
+- Tests cover these cases with `npm test` (`node --test`).
+
+## Requirement
+
+Standard: RFC 6901, JSON Pointer. The library must follow the published standard it implements.
